    ...
  }

  enc->iterEnd(obj, &tc);
  Buffer_AppendIndentNewlineUnchecked (enc);  // This is also called even if array is empty!
  Buffer_AppendIndentUnchecked (enc, enc->level);  // And this too!
  Buffer_AppendCharUnchecked (enc, ']');
  break;
}
```

The issue is clear - we always add newlines and indentation regardless of whether the container is empty.

The fix should be to:
1. Check if the container is empty first
2. Only add newlines if the container has items

One way to do this is to restructure the code to move the first newline into the loop, or to check if there are items before adding newlines.

